Checking the per-thread environment
===================================

make check runs two additional tests for every configured Lisp:

  <lisp>-thread-bindings.sh
    Always runs.  Checks all 31 required dynamic bindings and the fresh
    LINEARRAY, then runs 774 regression assertions.  These include a
    deliberately missing pair of bindings and 128 reproducible trials of
    nested environments, varied values and nonlocal exits.

  <lisp>-thread-race.sh
    Runs on SBCL built with threads and on native-threaded CCL.  On other
    Lisps it exits 77, which Automake reports as SKIP.  This says nothing
    about whether that Lisp has threads: only these two backends are
    implemented in this checker.  The portable binding test still runs.

The race uses two workers, each writing SIGN and checking its own answer.
Two semaphore rendezvous per round ensure both writes precede either read
and both reads precede the next write.  Over 2,000 rounds the unbound
control must lose exactly 2,000 of 4,000 answers; the bound run must lose
none.  Thus a scheduler that happens to run workers serially cannot give
a false pass.  Each worker counts privately.  Rendezvous have a ten-second
timeout, and worker errors are reported in the parent.  The unbound control
deliberately overwrites global SIGN; run the race in a fresh batch image,
as the driver does, rather than alongside an interactive computation.

Run the checks separately from the tests directory of a configured build:

  ./threadcheck.sh sbcl bindings
  ./threadcheck.sh sbcl race
  ./threadcheck.sh ccl64 bindings
  ./threadcheck.sh ccl64 race
  ./threadcheck.sh clisp bindings
  ./threadcheck.sh clisp race       # expected exit 77

Exit codes: 0 = pass, 1 = failed isolation or missing completion marker,
2 = a checker load/runtime error (including a regression assertion),
77 = unsupported race.  Launch failures retain
their nonzero exit code.  The driver also requires the exact PASS or SKIP
completion line: Maxima can auto-continue a Lisp load error and exit 0.
It passes the source path via an environment variable rather than inserting
it into Lisp source.  Separate source/build trees are supported; configure
retains its existing restrictions on directory names.  MAXIMA may name an
alternative maxima-local executable, including a path with spaces.

threadcheck-driver-test.sh checks 86 combinations of status, completion
marker and invalid arguments using a mock executable with spaces in its
path.  It also verifies temporary-log cleanup.  This runs under make check
without a Lisp of its own.

These tests cover the isolation claimed by WITH-THREAD-LOCAL-ENVIRONMENT.
They do not establish that the whole evaluator, the assumption database,
or share packages are safe for concurrent use.
